{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "\n\ndef is_palindrome(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\" Test if given string is a palindrome \"\"\"\n    return string == string[::-1]\n\n\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Find the shortest palindrome that begins with a supplied string.\n    Algorithm idea is simple:\n    - Find the longest postfix of supplied string that is a palindrome.\n    - Append to the end of the string reverse of a string prefix that comes before the palindromic suffix.\n    >>> make_palindrome('')\n    ''\n    >>> make_palindrome('cat')\n    'catac'\n    >>> make_palindrome('cata')\n    'catac'\n    \"\"\"\nHere's the completion of the `make_palindrome` function:\n\n```python\n    if not string:\n        return ''\n    \n    # Find the longest postfix that is a palindrome\n    for i in range(len(string)):\n        if is_palindrome(string[i:]):\n            # string[i:] is a palindromic suffix\n            # We need to append the reverse of string[:i] to the end\n            return string + string[:i][::-1]\n    \n    # If no palindromic suffix found (shouldn't happen as single char is palindrome)\n    return string + string[:-1][::-1]\n```\n\nThe algorithm works as follows:\n1. If the string is empty, return empty string\n2. Iterate through the string from left to right\n3. Check if the substring from position `i` to the end is a palindrome\n4. When we find the longest palindromic suffix (starting from `i`), we append the reverse of the prefix (characters before position `i`) to make the entire string a palindrome\n5. This gives us the shortest palindrome that begins with the original string"}
